}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120524/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120524"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616/selection/c6ff41ebdffa40bae588cc01861a18825e072116bd9c6b3e6bc03e8d4a5dd86f" :answer/id #uuid "5ba1b3fb-88dc-5145-9d8b-71fa80b76156" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{18}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616/selection/5bd506083b26258fa0a95592fb35bf47cdd382f7f42161f0c65d3072d0e5167a" :answer/id #uuid "898e332d-7c13-5eb8-b9f4-56b1be2a5743" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616/selection/2c556baeca22720839f2d5d5cdf7c2a3769cab998c78be79ed6cb243f90727c7" :answer/id #uuid "ac7ff837-3676-594f-b677-4f59b6a79b61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616/selection/b546c29e3eb561fca09ed32f3598ddb3cf90f00da3e078327b5c9bab102a6bda" :answer/id #uuid "16966b06-e665-5163-b6fa-eec16728002a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616/selection/df99d56d846c0bfd62e2553820ccb4af8b1a2a04a683f77fcd4158037e51e762" :answer/id #uuid "2d015b2e-df40-5d53-bcc5-015b0f87bfa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8fd6cc0-5195-5938-bda3-53708a6bd0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-5616/selection/b546c29e3eb561fca09ed32f3598ddb3cf90f00da3e078327b5c9bab102a6bda" :answer-field/choices ["q-5616/selection/c6ff41ebdffa40bae588cc01861a18825e072116bd9c6b3e6bc03e8d4a5dd86f" "q-5616/selection/5bd506083b26258fa0a95592fb35bf47cdd382f7f42161f0c65d3072d0e5167a" "q-5616/selection/2c556baeca22720839f2d5d5cdf7c2a3769cab998c78be79ed6cb243f90727c7" "q-5616/selection/b546c29e3eb561fca09ed32f3598ddb3cf90f00da3e078327b5c9bab102a6bda" "q-5616/selection/df99d56d846c0bfd62e2553820ccb4af8b1a2a04a683f77fcd4158037e51e762"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-5616" :question/id #uuid "d1ed0374-b4e0-5a94-b18c-7a2811425755" :question/math-academy-id "q-5616" :question/problem "A game show uses a roulette wheel divided into $50$ panels. There are $15$ blue, $15$ green, $15$ yellow, $2$ black, and $3$ white panels randomly shuffled on the roulette wheel. What is the probability that the roulette wheel stops on a white or green panel?" :question/worked-solution "Let $W$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a white panel, and let $G$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a green panel. Then the probability that the roulette stops on a white or green panel given by $P(W\\cup G).$\n\nGiven that the roulette will stop at only one panel, the events $W$ and $G$  are mutually exclusive, i.e., $P(W\\cap G)=0.$ \n\nWe can find $P(W)$ and $P(G)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $50$ panels in the roulette. Of those panels, $3$ are white, and $15$ are green. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =\\frac{3}{50}. \\\\ P(G) & =\\frac{15}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\cup G) & =P(W)+P(G)-P(W\\cap G) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{15}{50}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-5616/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-5616"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554/selection/5df1e722e09c055a88f8288a681d6644451c9bb1bc22bf81932e0643a8a535ff" :answer/id #uuid "36f094bd-4db2-58ba-92bd-7856fecd955e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14}{51}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554/selection/73bc73067af0e109dcf9e058c22416e7ffb00672d82aff0a3df3e15d733a7538" :answer/id #uuid "61018851-76ff-537b-84ec-0a0082791012" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{51}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554/selection/e2bcdb573fc9c43d47ef503047575afd3886f93a6eb15657d6fc19a2b63e9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "e672e55c-583b-594d-8997-3e2d055f54b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{52}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554/selection/f7f780e7dc7dd8ffa1f7015250b943c0c080db9fd2cdab4f2027ab8fa96fc07b" :answer/id #uuid "78f4c3bd-a10f-5ebd-b313-2b0c153883c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{52}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554/selection/cc000aed3b74797eb1b7faae113c6429953b3f4fe02ff94412d001685ebf50ad" :answer/id #uuid "9e04b427-0c9d-5679-b48c-6e1ab9bbeb07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8be57220-224e-59b9-9572-6140d3506fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-12554/selection/cc000aed3b74797eb1b7faae113c6429953b3f4fe02ff94412d001685ebf50ad" :answer-field/choices ["q-12554/selection/5df1e722e09c055a88f8288a681d6644451c9bb1bc22bf81932e0643a8a535ff" "q-12554/selection/73bc73067af0e109dcf9e058c22416e7ffb00672d82aff0a3df3e15d733a7538" "q-12554/selection/e2bcdb573fc9c43d47ef503047575afd3886f93a6eb15657d6fc19a2b63e9d2d" "q-12554/selection/f7f780e7dc7dd8ffa1f7015250b943c0c080db9fd2cdab4f2027ab8fa96fc07b" "q-12554/selection/cc000aed3b74797eb1b7faae113c6429953b3f4fe02ff94412d001685ebf50ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-12554" :question/id #uuid "ffaa74bf-70c5-5164-b2b4-295879e59db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-12554" :question/problem "A card is drawn from a standard $52$-card deck. The drawn card is the Ace of Spades and it is set aside. A second card is drawn from the deck. What is the probability that the drawn card is an ace or a spade?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits.*" :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The second card drawn is an ace,\" and $S$ the event \"The second card drawn is a spade.\" The required probability is given by  $P(A\\cup S).$ \n\nGiven that the Ace of Spades is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $S$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap S)=0.$\n\nSince the Ace of Spades was set aside, there are $51$ cards remaining,  of which $3$ are aces and $12$ are spades. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{3}{51}, \\\\ P(S) & =\\frac{12}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup S) & =P(A)+P(S)-P(A\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{51}+\\frac{12}{51}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+12}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-12554/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-12554"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526/selection/0064d374f806cba8ea43a11831d2980495c6733de7e909ec06431bd236f4f2b3" :answer/id #uuid "99a0c687-95d8-5749-bf9e-2f9b63990362" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "5dbef13e-6dd1-5c06-b65f-4371e3c5491a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "35c2cf11-17d7-5c10-87ae-5e0a09d47cc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526/selection/0417788a5409ad5447fb6d379532fc1bfef99c80e1411a914d6ff9762ba8f7ac" :answer/id #uuid "3737965e-5af6-5c5d-b9a1-50982b7e5dae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526/selection/ea886782f883139045e5abc122fc35c608bfcc0ac4d51b469ff658e915045408" :answer/id #uuid "8b8fc34b-21b2-5295-be25-cccf6cde47ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "66c06ba9-bee7-5cd5-abe0-ed4fe50771c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120526/selection/0064d374f806cba8ea43a11831d2980495c6733de7e909ec06431bd236f4f2b3" :answer-field/choices ["q-120526/selection/0064d374f806cba8ea43a11831d2980495c6733de7e909ec06431bd236f4f2b3" "q-120526/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" "q-120526/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-120526/selection/0417788a5409ad5447fb6d379532fc1bfef99c80e1411a914d6ff9762ba8f7ac" "q-120526/selection/ea886782f883139045e5abc122fc35c608bfcc0ac4d51b469ff658e915045408"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120526" :question/id #uuid "1db7a6ec-d7f9-50c5-b3f9-3a121c39ded3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120526" :question/problem "A number is picked from $10$ to $19.$ What is the probability of picking an even number or a prime number?" :question/worked-solution "Let $E$ be the event that the picked number is an even number, and let $R$ be the event that the picked number is a prime number. Then the probability that the selected number is an even number or a prime number is $P(E\\cup R).$\n\nGiven that all prime numbers between $10$ and $19$ are odd, $E$ and $R$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(E\\cap R)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(E)$ and $P(R)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $10$ numbers from  $10$ to $19$, of which  $5$ are even, and $4$ are prime.   Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E) & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ P(R) & =\\frac{4}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E\\cup R) & =P(E)+P(R)-P(E\\cap R) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{4}{10}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545/selection/205cba6731175357a41a0726f161ccb413fc9be16c07fd5fff93d27dbc73eebe" :answer/id #uuid "12bfca1f-8635-5a87-82b6-b210263fcc07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{19}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545/selection/09f9568b9032a258744b73b08553a45679ce3baf10e1847349ddb9cf78a3d261" :answer/id #uuid "69e0b292-fe54-54c9-bc4a-4aed2502b682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545/selection/0417788a5409ad5447fb6d379532fc1bfef99c80e1411a914d6ff9762ba8f7ac" :answer/id #uuid "cb7efbc7-86e4-51d5-a953-2439a4a18d19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545/selection/c0adf4cf464bed156302d2dcd699f1fedcb3affada15861e2f031805b6c64bef" :answer/id #uuid "723c0850-fa31-5c6c-b242-e21ed2e6de9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{19}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545/selection/f594cbbb4a66bc41743b074d8df31c04839068c3f93c44ebcfba00b647948a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ccf521c5-9549-5401-bf47-24f4b8a631f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf423359-8d32-5275-ba24-80d4806e070a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120545/selection/205cba6731175357a41a0726f161ccb413fc9be16c07fd5fff93d27dbc73eebe" :answer-field/choices ["q-120545/selection/205cba6731175357a41a0726f161ccb413fc9be16c07fd5fff93d27dbc73eebe" "q-120545/selection/09f9568b9032a258744b73b08553a45679ce3baf10e1847349ddb9cf78a3d261" "q-120545/selection/0417788a5409ad5447fb6d379532fc1bfef99c80e1411a914d6ff9762ba8f7ac" "q-120545/selection/c0adf4cf464bed156302d2dcd699f1fedcb3affada15861e2f031805b6c64bef" "q-120545/selection/f594cbbb4a66bc41743b074d8df31c04839068c3f93c44ebcfba00b647948a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120545" :question/id #uuid "e9f5f88f-94fe-59a3-858c-68b6a4ea8014" :question/math-academy-id "q-120545" :question/problem "On a shelf, there are $11$ novels and $9$ science books. $1$ of the novels and $3$ of the science books are illustrated. The illustrated novel is taken from the shelf and set apart. If a single book is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a novel or an illustrated book?" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ be the event that the selected book is an illustrated book, and let $N$ be the event that it is a novel. Then the probability that the selected book is a novel or an illustrated book is given by $P(I\\cup N).$\n\nSince the only illustrated novel was removed, there are no novels left on the shelf that are also illustrated. Therefore, $I$ and $N$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(I\\cap N)=0.$\n\nSince the illustrated novel was set apart, there are $10+9=19$ books remaining, of which $10$ are novels and $3$ are illustrated. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I) & =\\frac{3}{19}, \\\\ P(N) & =\\frac{10}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I\\cup N) & =P(I)+P(N)-P(I\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{19}+\\frac{10}{19}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+10}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120545/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120545"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-5617"] :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the name starts with an \"R,\" and let $E$ be the event that the name starts with an \"E.\" Then the probability that the name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\" is given by $P(R\\cup E).$\n\nGiven that only one letter can be picked as the first letter, $R$ and $E$ are mutually exclusive events. i.e. $P(R\\cap E)=0.$\n\nThere are $26$ letters in the alphabet, and each letter has an equal chance of being randomly picked ($1$ out of $26$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =\\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ P(E) & =\\frac{1}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}+\\frac{1}{26}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5727"] :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{7}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3-7}{12} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5634"] :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9},$ find $P(B).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5636"] :question/problem "Two cards are drawn from a standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c1a5898916d3156aa9acb1884f2ab9fd149c10ca2149574a50fc6147586691e6.png)-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set aside, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?\n\n*Hint: A standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/5f378bdc2525ccdfd98ae36dc698e3bdf3f328a76d6841faf2198ec37b34db9a.png)-card deck has ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d44ac804f533a60a0d252fbcddaec9693d8b989dc429bf03f3ef1f0587cf3453.png) suits with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/f3ae95c18ff411808c146e5d3bfc30f49380b8272511f1a90a3400209b2df340.png) cards in each suit.There is one ace in each suit.\n Hearts is one of the suits.*" :question/worked-solution "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/15fa693fff5bc0eb2a8851089612b5a3fb969c3fd5fbc7c0821e58e52ff4260d.png) be the event that the third card is an ace, and let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/86056525c7e84fd98e233f12873f4b7bd6ed728224c7c1afe986743f6cda0aa0.png) be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a143776333b936a72708b71c7deadb626e347a6c094630c9dbce894d550ed5bb.png)\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d18d46bb8861930c4c3e602e4eef58228e745671a5445823a11d088e0a9e43a8.png) are mutually exclusive events, i.e., ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/37f1cba75404eaf490f8ae5f3de37c1da606e372b6b2e1f1c9c2c23a9ee0f82d.png)\n\nWe can find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3abd50b1d24b25f8908834ec0b3ef94c630f1c8c5352c0528a381b4bddc30ed1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a73f6c30e5a00859bc7f04c5f08a59a978dcc714634187dd178a9fa2059d1578.png) by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards in the deck. Among the remaining ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/97314fc826b205001aa477df5cccb4ceb42aff037d8ccf663a40341a70629d9f.png) hearts and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/6da8ea22dec2b00f0f9fa815118a83e3877bfdba5149ea487f242a1839878b0e.png) aces. Therefore:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/ecf686508e7fe0e4454085c9af1c06ce613ebf9dc1ef4c5bc2f0f93a146a8a23.png)\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/456f4dde4ad17f8738a277bb3a6eb139db35bb20aaa85dba794f2f1730bd1495.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
